# Composing eight bars: a phrase that asks and a phrase that answers, bars that add up, a tune that
# moves mostly by step, and a motif (the old city clock's chime) repeated and moved. The last
# question is the child's own tune, heard by a grown-up.
lesson music-composing-eight-bars v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=8 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Composing eight bars"
  goal "Write eight bars that ask a question and answer it, ending on the home note."

  look {
    scene 36x28 {
      row top space=1 align=end at=canvas(1, 1) {
        clocktower clock hour=6 minute=0
        column staves space=0 align=start {
          text q "Bars 1 and 2, a question"
          notes asks clef=treble notes=[C4, C4, G4, G4, A4, A4, G4] values=[1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2] meter=0
          text a "Bars 3 and 4, an answer"
          notes answers clef=treble notes=[F4, F4, E4, E4, D4, D4, C4] values=[1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2] meter=0
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      At six o'clock the clock tower in the old city chimes, and Ana wants to write a tune of her
      own. Most tunes are built like Twinkle Twinkle: one phrase asks a question and ends away from
      home, on G, and the next answers it and lands on C, the home note, with a long note. An
      eight-bar tune is four bars that ask and four that answer. Every bar adds up to four beats,
      the tune moves mostly by step, and a small idea, like the clock's chime, can come back in a new
      place.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice music.compose-home-ending count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    level easy {
      worked music.compose-fill-bar b=0 level=medium
    }
    practice music.compose-fill-bar count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    show music.compose-play-chime
    show music.compose-fill-bar b=1 hard-b=3
    practice music.compose-sequence count=1 seed=3 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice music.compose-leaps count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    show music.compose-tap-end
    level hard {
      show music.compose-check-bars m=3
    }
    practice music.oldcity-market-seconds count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.compose-check-bars m=1 easy-m=0 hard-m=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.compose-eight-bars v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Ask a question that ends away from home, then answer it and land on the home note."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a short tune is built from phrases that pair up, a question that ends away from
    the home note and an answer that lands on it, and that the craft is in the small things: every
    bar adding up, mostly steps with a few leaps, and one idea coming back, sometimes moved to a new
    place. The wrong ideas to watch for are bars that do not add up because a dot or a quaver was
    miscounted, and an answer that stops on the wrong note, which sounds unfinished. In the working,
    look for the child adding each bar before moving on and singing the phrase to hear where it
    ends. The two-star question checks two bars against the rules; the three-star question is the
    child's own tune, which you mark by listening against what the page asks, and it is not marked
    by the page. A child who is stuck can start from the chime and change one note at a time, and
    go back to the way in; stay on this lesson another day if the bars still do not add up. Off the
    page, make up a question on a keyboard or by singing and let the child answer it.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the phrases to judge come from Twinkle Twinkle, the bar to finish is worked once in the teacher's pen before two in four-four, the chime is moved one step up, the tune to read has one leap, the band plays the tune once at 120 or 80 beats a minute, the two-star's bar 7 has a beat too many, and the three-star is four bars of the child's own."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the phrase comes from the Ode to Joy, with its dotted note, or Mary Had a Little Lamb, one bar to finish is in three-four, the chime moves up a 3rd, the tune to read has four leaps, a bar 8 of three beats is found before the two-star, whose bars have nothing wrong, the band plays the tune twice, and the three-star asks for bar 5 to be the chime moved up and no more than three leaps."
  }
}
